//? Title: RegNet: A Quantized Width Rule and Complete X/Y Classifiers
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
RegNet generates stage dimensions from a compact quantized linear rule. We inspect that
rule, compare packed and split grouped-filter derivatives, verify all fourteen released budgets,
then explicitly fit the full Y-400MF CIFAR network with tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.4_efficient_and_mobile_architectures/regnet.cpp`.

> The method

For block index j, RegNet starts with a linear continuous width, quantizes it to multiplicative
steps, then aligns it to eight:

$$u_j=w_0+w_a j,\qquad k_j=\operatorname{round}\!\left(\frac{\log(u_j/w_0)}{\log w_m}\right),\qquad
 q_j=8\operatorname{round}\!\left(\frac{w_0 w_m^{k_j}}8\right).$$
\alttext(A small set of design scalars generates repeated widths and therefore stage depths.)

The reference uses Float32 arithmetic and ties-to-even rounding. Consecutive equal q values
form stages; their widths are adjusted to a compatible group width min(g,width). The effective
3×3 group COUNT is output_width/group_width. This distinction matters: the table's g is channels
per group. These calculations are architecture metadata, with no learned predictions or fitting.
The helper temporarily selects nearest rounding in the caller thread and restores the previous
mode, including when width generation throws. All outputs must remain integer-representable.

Y-400MF has d=16,w0=48,wa=27.89,wm=2.09 and group width eight, giving stages
48/104/208/440 with depths 1/3/6/6. X-400MF has widths 32/64/160/400 and depths
1/2/7/12. The fourteen released X/Y designs below check every stage width/depth, full learned
parameter count and moving-state count. Full weight models run at 64 pixels to bound computation;
224-pixel convolution output sides scale EXACTLY by 224/64 because both geometries divide
through all five downsamplings. Pooled SE and classifier costs stay fixed. X/Y-400MF also execute
actual 224-pixel forwards to validate this explicit target-budget calculation. The report does not
claim all fourteen large models were forwarded at 224 pixels or timed.

The stem is 32-channel 3×3/2/BN/ReLU. Each stage starts with stride two, including stage one.
Bottlenecks use dense 1×1/BN/ReLU, grouped 3×3/BN/ReLU, optional SE, linear 1×1/BN,
shortcut sum and ReLU. Projection shortcuts have 1×1/BN. X omits SE; Y pools spatially,
reduces to lround(.25*INPUT_channels) with ReLU, expands with logistic sigmoid, then scales
channels. The reduction is not .25 times output width. Global mean and a biased classifier finish
the model. There is no dropout. He-uniform fan-in is an initialization adaptation; BN learns
scale/shift with epsilon 1e-5. Full X/Y-400MF have 5495976/4344144 learned scalars.
The packed grouped helper preserves independent per-group learned leaves; four Float64
fixtures compare every derivative against split filtering at stride/dilation two.

The full-width Y-400MF backward probe keeps all sixteen blocks at 64 pixels, shifts BN betas/SE
biases and checks finite nonzero gradients for every parameter tensor. Unlike ungated additive
streams, ReLU after every sum prevents the same unconditional constant-shift cancellation claim.
Moving updates are disabled only for that diagnostic. MAC budgets count convolution, pooled SE
and classifier products, excluding BN, pooling, activation/addition and memory traffic. One MAC
is a multiply-accumulate pair; the values are not measured runtime or counts of individual FLOPs.

The visible fit changes only the first stem stride and class count for original 32×32 CIFAR images.
All four stage downsamplings remain. It has 3907554 learned/27152 moving scalars and uses
64 fit/32 validation/32 official-test rows, 12 batch-eight epochs (96 updates, kept short so
the report builds quickly), Adam(.001), explicit kernel
L2=.0001/2 and independent padded crops/flips. Validation selects paired parameters/moving
moments before test scoring. This bounded single-seed experiment differs from the larger source
fit and published ImageNet schedules; it cannot establish design-space or benchmark superiority.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/regnet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/regnet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/regnet_concept.tikz > build/regnet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/regnet_concept.svg build/regnet_concept.dvi > build/regnet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
